Regenerated against l2fwd/harness.sh, which absorbed counter_groups.sh and
saturation.sh. Latency half already landed (G_LATENCY, `latency` group); patch
now carries coherence half only.

Adds the two resources §5.27 enumerated the machine against but never listed:
DRAM *latency* as distinct from bandwidth, and inter-core coherence traffic.

WHY THESE TWO, AND WHY NOW
--------------------------
§5.31 found that at q=23 the core is 58.4% memory-bound while every instrument
measuring a memory *resource* reads 1-5%: DRAM bandwidth 4.1%, L3-miss stalls
1.2%, fill buffers 1.0%, page walker 1.0%. That combination cannot be a
bandwidth or a capacity limit. Latency that is not being hidden, and cores
interfering with each other, are the two candidates left, and neither is
instrumented.

DRAM LATENCY COMES FREE FROM LITTLE'S LAW
-----------------------------------------
No counter reports memory latency directly. But the group already collects
OFFCORE_REQUESTS_OUTSTANDING.DATA_RD (event 0x20, umask 0x08) in G_FB. Adding
OFFCORE_REQUESTS.DATA_RD (event 0x21, umask 0x08) gives

    mean latency in core cycles = outstanding / requests

which is Little's law with no modelling assumption beyond the counters being
what they claim. This is why latency gets its own group rather than joining
G_FB: the two 0x2x events plus FB_FULL's 0x48 in one group risks the same
scheduler collision §5.27 documents for three simultaneous 0x48-family events.

*** THESE ENCODINGS ARE UNVALIDATED AND MUST NOT BE TRUSTED YET ***
Per §5.23, perf has no JSON event file for family 6 model 207, so a generic
alias can map to nothing and read zero -- indistinguishable from a real zero.
Every encoding below must be checked against a workload with a known answer
before any number from it is quoted.

  latency:   bench_membw's dependent pointer chase already IS the known answer.
             MLP must be ~1 there, so outstanding/requests must come out at
             roughly one full memory latency (order 200-400 cycles on this
             part). If it reads 0, or reads ~1, the encoding is wrong.

  coherence: THERE IS NO KNOWN-ANSWER WORKLOAD FOR THIS TODAY. bench_membw has
             a sequential stream, a pointer chase and a register-only chain --
             none of them share a line between cores, so all three must read
             ~0 on the XSNP counters, which only proves the counters are not
             stuck high. Validating that they are not stuck LOW needs a new
             bench_membw arm: two threads ping-ponging a single shared line,
             where every access must miss to a modified line in another core's
             cache and the XSNP_FWD count must approach the access count.
             That arm does not exist and is the prerequisite for this half of
             the patch. Do not run the coherence group against l2fwd before it
             does.

The 0xd2 umask assignments in particular differ across microarchitectures; they
are written here from the Golden Cove server encoding and are the single most
likely thing in this file to be wrong.

--- a/l2fwd/harness.sh
+++ b/l2fwd/harness.sh
@@ -98,6 +98,23 @@
 cpu/event=0x20,umask=0x08,name=offcore_reqs_outstanding_data_rd/,\
 cpu/event=0x21,umask=0x08,name=offcore_reqs_data_rd/"
 
+# --- inter-core coherence: are the workers fighting over lines? -----------
+# A per-core resource holds its utilisation flat as cores are added; a shared
+# one climbs. Coherence traffic does neither -- it GROWS with core count
+# because it is created by the cores themselves, which is the shape §5.31
+# sees and cannot currently name.
+#
+# MEM_LOAD_L3_HIT_RETIRED.XSNP_* counts loads that hit in L3 and required a
+# cross-core snoop. XSNP_FWD is the expensive one: the line was modified in
+# another core's cache and had to be forwarded.
+#
+# NOT VALIDATED, AND NOT VALIDATABLE with the current bench_membw. See the
+# header of this patch: a shared-line ping-pong arm must be added first.
+G_COHERENCE="cycles,\
+cpu/event=0xd2,umask=0x01,name=mem_load_l3_hit_xsnp_miss/,\
+cpu/event=0xd2,umask=0x02,name=mem_load_l3_hit_xsnp_no_fwd/,\
+cpu/event=0xd2,umask=0x04,name=mem_load_l3_hit_xsnp_fwd/"
+
 # --- DRAM bandwidth (uncore, per socket; must be collected system-wide) ----
 # A separate PMU from the core counters, so this does not compete with them for
 # general-purpose counters and may be collected alongside.
@@ -583,6 +600,7 @@
     fb)       echo "$G_FB" ;;
     tlbmem)   echo "$G_TLBMEM" ;;
     latency)  echo "$G_LATENCY" ;;
+    coherence) echo "$G_COHERENCE" ;;
     bw)       echo "$G_BW" ;;
     *) echo "FATAL: unknown group $1" >&2; exit 1 ;;
   esac
